# Deliberately narrow. This repo has always relied on git's default handling for its
# source files, and `core.autocrlf = true` is set locally, so a blanket `* text=auto` here
# would renormalise the whole tree in one commit. Only the shell entrypoints are pinned.

# Git hooks MUST stay LF even on Windows checkouts. With autocrlf on and no rule here, git
# rewrites .githooks/pre-push to CRLF on checkout, and a CRLF shell script breaks in ways
# that are easy to miss: `case "$1" in` becomes `in<CR>` and dies with "word unexpected"
# under dash, and a trailing-backslash line continuation escapes the CR instead of the
# newline so the command silently splits in two. The old fail-open hook happened to survive
# CRLF because it used neither construct; that was luck, not design. Verified 2026-08-04.
.githooks/** text eol=lf

# Pre-emptive: same reasoning for any shell script added later. There is deliberately no
# .sh in the tree right now — the pre-push self-test was one for a single commit and is now
# scripts/pre-push-selftest.mjs, because npm on Windows runs scripts through cmd.exe where
# no `sh` is on PATH. Node driver, no shell, no CRLF exposure. The rule stays so the next
# .sh someone adds is born correct.
*.sh text eol=lf
